rce><year>2003</year><volume>31</volume><fpage>e15</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12582260</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/nar/gng015</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B19"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gentleman</surname><given-names>RC</given-names></name><name><surname>Carey</surname><given-names>VJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Bates</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name><name><surname>Bolstad</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Dettling</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Dudoit</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Ellis</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Gautier</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Ge</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Gentry</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Hornik</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Hothorn</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Huber</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Iacus</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Irizarry</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Leisch</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Maechler</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Rossini</surname><given-names>AJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Sawitzki</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Smyth</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Tierney</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>JY</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhang</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Bioconductor: open software development for computational biology and bioinformatics</article-title><source>Genome Biol</source><year>2004</year><volume>5</volume><fpage>R80</fpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15461798</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1186/gb-2004-5-10-r80</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B20"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Barrett</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Suzek</surname><given-names>TO</given-names></name><name><surname>Troup</surname><given-names>DB</given-names></name><name><surname>Wilhite</surname><given-names>SE</given-names></name><name><surname>Ngau</surname><given-names>WC</given-names></name><name><surname>Ledoux</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Rudnev</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Lash</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><name><surname>Fujibuchi</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Edgar</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>NCBI GEO: mining millions of expression profiles--database and tools</article-title><source>Nucleic Acids Res</source><year>2005</year><volume>33</volume><fpage>D562</fpage><lpage>6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15608262</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1093/nar/gki022</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B21"><citation citation-type="other"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bolstad</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Model based QC Assessment of Affymetrix GeneChips</article-title><source>Bioconductor Vignettes</source><year>2005</year><ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.bioconductor.org/docs/vignettes.html"/></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>NUSE boxplot. </bold>After RMA preprocessing, the standard errors were standardized across arrays and then plotted to assess the data quality. x axis: the first letter "Or, Neb and Yrk" stands for three laboratories from the centers of Oregon, Nebraska and Yerkes, respectively. The second letter "B, F, P, T, H" represents the tissues of Brain (Cerebral Cortex), Fibroblast, Pancreas, Testis and Thymus, respectively. The third letter "1 and 2" are the two replicated measurements for each RNA extract.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>Heat Map Plot of Array-array Correlation Coefficients. </bold>The Spearman correlation coefficient was computed to quantify the degree of similarity between each pair of arrays. For the array name, "Or, Neb and Yrk" stands for three laboratories from the centers of Oregon, Nebraska and Yerkes, respectively while "B, F, P, T, H" represent the tissues of Cortex, Fibroblast, Pancreas, Testis and Thymus, respectively. "1 and 2" are the two replicated measurements for each RNA extract.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><bold>Boxplot of the difference between sample replicates</bold>. After RMA preprocessing, probeset signal differences between sample replicates were plotted to assesss precision. x axis: the first letters "Or, Neb and Yrk" stand for three laboratories from the centers of Oregon, Nebraska and Yerkes, respectively. The second letters "B, F, P, T, H" represent Brain (Cerebral Cortex), Fibroblast, Pancreas, Testis and Thymus, respectively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p><bold>Venn diagram of the 500 most differentially expressed genes across three laboratories. </bold>The SAM algorithm was applied to arrays from each laboratory, preprocessed by RMA, to select the 500 most differentially expressed genes for each center. This Venn diagram shows the number of genes that were found in common among the centers.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><bold>Pairwise CAT plot. </bold>Each of the dashed lines represents CAT curves that indicate the agreement between pairs of laboratories while the solid line indicates the agreement across alll three laboratories. These results were obtained by plotting the proportion of common genes between laboratories (Y axis) and the number of differentially expressed genes (X axis).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><bold>Agreement within genes for fold changes determined by Affymetrix rhesus GeneChip and quantitative real-time PCR. </bold>The "rhesus-array Fold Change" is defined as: Mean Signal of the target gene in its exclusively-expressed tissue/Mean Signal of the target gene in the other tissue, while the gene signal was estimated from the Affymetrix rhesus arrays. The "qRT-PCR Fold change" is defined as: 2<sup>&#x00394;&#x00394;Ct </sup>&#x00394;&#x00394;Ct = (Ct of the target gene in its exclusively-expressed tissue &#x02013; Ct of the control gene in its exclusively-expressed tissue) &#x02013; (Ct of the target gene in the other tissue &#x02013; Ct of the control gene in the other tissue), while the threshold cycle (Ct) was the average value from five replicates from the qRT-PCR assay. Please note the Ct value of the gene PRSS2 in the tissue of Fibroblast was missing due to the undetermined readings. Thus, the maximum cycle number 40 was assigned to approximate the value.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p><bold>Comparison of Percent Present calls of rhesus samples hybridized with human and rhesus macaque GeneChips. </bold>Percent Present called after hybridization of the rhesus samples with the human Affymetrix GeneChip (hgu133plus2) (dark bars) and the rhesus macaque GeneChip (light bars). Y &#x02013; axis indicates the percent of total probesets scored present using the standard Affymetrix default parameters. X &#x02013; axis indicates the results obtained with each rhesus macaque sample. "B, F, P, T, H" represent Brain (Cerebral Cortex), Fibroblast, Pancreas, Testis and Thymus, respectively. Numbers represent each of two replicates for each tissue sample.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2164-8-61-7"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Scaling factors (SF) of 30 arrays hybridized to Affymetrix GeneChips. Table 1 presents the scaling factors (SF) of all 30 arrays, where SF was calculated by setting up the Target Intensity (TGT) to be 100.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody><tr><td align="left">Oregon</td><td align="left">Array</td><td align="left">Cor1</td><td align="left">Cor2</td><td align="left">Fib1</td><td align="left">Fib2</td><td align="left">Pan1</td><td align="left">Pan2</td><td align="left">Tes1</td><td align="left">Tes1</td><td align="left">Thy1</td><td align="left">Thy2</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">SF</td><td align="left">1.29</td><td align="left">1.19</td><td align="left">0.96</td><td align="left">1.22</td><td align="left">3.60</td><td align="left">3.54</td><td align="left">0.91</td><td align="left">0.95</td><td align="left">1.05</td><td align="left">1.04</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Nebraska</td><td align="left">Array</td><td align="left">Br1</td><td align="left">Br2</td><td align="left">Fib1</td><td align="left">Fib2</td><td align="left">Pan1</td><td align="left">Pan2</td><td align="left">Tes1</td><td align="left">Tes1</td><td align="left">Thy1</td><td align="left">Thy2</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">SF</td><td align="left">0.92</td><td align="left">1.09</td><td align="left">1.05</td><td align="left">1.20</td><td align="left">4.58</td><td align="left">3.88</td><td align="left">0.94</td><td align="left">0.77</td><td align="left">1.28</td><td align="left">1.27</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Yerkes</td><td align="left">Array</td><td align="left">Br1</td><td align="left">Br2</td><td align="left">Fib1</td><td align="left">Fib2</td><td align="left">Pan1</td><td align="left">Pan2</td><td align="left">Tes1</td><td align="left">Tes1</td><td align="left">Thy1</td><td align="left">Thy2</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">SF</td><td align="left">1.13</td><td align="left">0.84</td><td align="left">0.65</td><td align="left">1.09</td><td align="left">1.75</td><td align="left">2.13</td><td align="left">0.89</td><td align="left">0.75</td><td align="left">0.92</td><td align="left">0.80</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><p>Cor = cererbral cortex, Fib = fibroblast, Pan = pancreas, Tes = testis and Thy = thymus. 1 and 2 are two technical replicates.</p></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="T2"><label>Table 2</label><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody><tr><td align="left"><underline>Gene</underline></td><td></td></tr><tr><td align="left">SNAP25</td><td align="left"><underline>Forward primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">TGCTGTGAAACAGTGTGGATGTAAA</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Reverse primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">GCAACAAAGAATTGTACTGATAACAAACCA</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Probe</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">ACTAACCACCATTTCCC</td></tr><tr><td align="left">TKTL1</td><td align="left"><underline>Forward primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">GAATTGTAGATTGCAGTAGCCTAGGT</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Reverse primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">GGAACAGCGGTAGGTGTCATG</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Probe</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">CCACAGCACAATAACA</td></tr><tr><td align="left">PRSS2</td><td align="left"><underline>Forward primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">GCTGAGTGTGAAGCCTCCTA</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Reverse primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">AGGAAGCCCACACAGAACATG</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Probe</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">TTGCTGGTAATCTTCC</td></tr><tr><td align="left">CD8A</td><td align="left"><underline>Forward primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">GGCAGACCTGACTCTCTTAAGGA</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Reverse primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">CAGGCATTGCTTGTACCATACTGTA</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Probe</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">CTGCTGTCCCAAACAC</td></tr><tr><td align="left">GREM1</td><td align="left"><underline>Forward primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">TGCAGATTTGGCTCAAGTAAAGAGA</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Reverse primer</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">GCTGTGCTTTTAGGGTAGTTATGCT</td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left"><underline>Probe</underline></td></tr><tr><td></td><td align="left">CTCCACTGGAATAACC</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec></back></article>